Here's my honest, opinionated ranking. The deeper research changed one big thing in my thinking: the topic being "hot" is actually a risk, not an advantage. SIH 2025 had ~72,000 idea submissions for ~271 problem statements — glamorous AI
  statements (like FloatChat) attracted enormous competition, while data-driven strategy analyses (https://www.scribd.com/document/903478555/Detailed-Report-on-SIH2025-Choosing-problem-statement) show niche-ministry statements (NTRO, Textiles,
  Chemicals, Coal, Ayush) often get ~50 teams vs 500+ for MeitY/Health. Your real formula is: RAG/LLM skills (which judges now heavily favor) applied to an unglamorous ministry's data problem.

  My ranked picks:

  1. Scheme-Eligibility Discovery Agent ("Sarkari Saathi") — my top pick

  Win probability: highest. Implementation: easy-medium.
  RAG over myScheme's ~3,000 schemes → conversational eligibility interview in Hindi/English/one regional language → ranked eligible schemes + document checklist. Why I rank it #1: the data is public and clean (myScheme is scrapable/structured
  — no messy NetCDF or sensor data), the demo is emotionally powerful in 3 minutes ("a widowed farmer discovers 7 schemes she didn't know about"), impact is instantly obvious to government judges, and the whole thing is buildable by a 6-person
  team in well under 36 hours with a standard stack (embeddings + vector DB + LLM + Bhashini translation). It maps to rural development / social justice ministry statements, which are less saturated than MeitY.

  2. Conversational Analytics over Groundwater Data ("JalGPT")

  Win probability: high — proven pattern. Implementation: medium.
  Text-to-SQL + RAG over INGRES/DWLR data with auto-generated charts. This exact shape has produced winners two years running (INGRES chatbot 2024, DWLR evaluation 2025 (https://x.com/CGWB_CHQ/status/1999389032535806266)), and Jal Shakti will
  almost certainly repost a variant. The risk: because it's proven, competition is now moderate-high. Your differentiator must be the agentic query-planning + visualization layer, and pre-loading real CGWB data before the finale. Pick this if
  your team is strong on data engineering.

  3. Multilingual Legal-Aid & Grievance RAG ("NyayaSetu")

  Win probability: high. Implementation: easy-medium.
  RAG over the new criminal codes (BNS/BNSS/BSA) + Constitution + grievance categories, answering in plain language with exact section citations, drafting complaint text. The new codes are a genuine freshness hook — most citizens and even
  police are still confused by the IPC→BNS mapping, and a section-mapper alone is a demo moment. Legal texts are perfectly clean RAG corpora (structured, sectioned, public). Department of Justice statements are less contested than MeitY ones.
  Guardrails ("I cite, I don't advise") impress government judges.

  4. RAG Assistant for a Niche PSU's Technical Documentation

  Win probability: very high per-team odds. Implementation: medium. The contrarian pick.
  Every year, PSUs like Coal India, NTPC, GAIL, Railways (RDSO), DRDO/NTRO post "AI system to search/summarize our manuals, safety circulars, inspection reports" statements that get a fraction of the applicants. It's the same RAG stack as topic
  #1 but pointed at, say, mine-safety regulations or railway maintenance manuals. Less glamorous, dramatically better odds — this is what the strategy data (https://reskilll.com/blogs/sih-2026-problem-statements-how-to-choose-right-one-team/)
  actually supports. When the 2026 problem statements drop, filter for low submitted-idea counts on RAG-shaped PSU statements and pounce.

  5. Public Health Chatbot with Outbreak Alerts ("Arogya Sahayak")

  Win probability: medium-high. Implementation: easy.
  It was a real 2025 statement and will likely recur, and it's the easiest build on this list (curated health corpus + WhatsApp interface + Bhashini). But health attracts heavy competition, so you only win with hard differentiation:
  citation-only answers, refusal-to-diagnose guardrails, IVR/voice for non-smartphone users, and live IDSP outbreak integration. Choose this only if your team can nail the safety story.

  6. NEP-2020 Career & Curriculum Advisor ("ShikshaMitra")

  Win probability: medium. Implementation: easy.
  Career advisory literally won at SIH 2025 (https://eduadvice.in/educational-news-details/smart-india-hackathon-2025-winners-announced/9101), which proves judges like it — but that also means 2026 will see many copies. I rank it last of the
  six: easiest to build, hardest to differentiate. Viable mainly for state-government problem statements at less-contested nodal centres.

  My bottom-line advice: commit to topics #1 and #4 as your strategy pair. Build the generic multilingual-RAG-with-citations engine now (it's the same core for all six), then when SIH 2026 problem statements are released (~August–September),
  check the official "Submitted Ideas Count" column and slot your engine into whichever matching statement has the lowest competition. Past winners consistently say answering every word of the official PS
  (https://www.anishprashun.me/blog/sih-2025-tips-and-common-mistakes) and having a working demo beat topic glamour — and remember your first battle is the internal college hackathon
  (https://velonx.in/blog/sih-2026-first-timers-guide-everything-you-need-to-know-before-you-register), not the finale.

